      "comments": [
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-07-24T08:53:40Z",
          "note": "No Critical or High security findings. All OWASP Top 10 categories reviewed against the new handleGetChunkText() handler and its server.ts route block. Full review summary below."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-07-24T08:53:40Z",
          "note": "A01 — Broken Access Control: PASS. slug and repoUrlParam are validated by SAFE_SLUG_REGEX (/^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]*$/) in server.ts before any handler call, and again by assertSafeSlug() inside handleGetChunkText(). filename is validated against CHUNK_FILENAME_RE (/^[A-Za-z0-9_-]+\\.jsonl$/) — a strict allowlist that rejects path separators, dots except the .jsonl suffix, and all traversal sequences. The derived mdPath is then checked with startsWith(chunksDir + sep) — a defense-in-depth boundary check so no derived path can escape the chunks directory. No IDOR vectors identified: the route requires a validated (repo, slug, filename) triple and resolves the on-disk path entirely server-side."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-07-24T08:53:40Z",
          "note": "A02 — Cryptographic Failures: PASS. No encryption or secrets involved. .md and .jsonl files are plain text at rest and served over a local HTTP server (same trust boundary as all other endpoints in this codebase). No passwords, tokens, or keys are written or read."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-07-24T08:53:40Z",
          "note": "A03 — Injection: PASS. No user input is interpolated into shell commands, SQL, template engines, or OS-level calls. readFile() and writeFile() both receive server-derived absolute paths (validated, not user-supplied strings). renderChunksToText() receives raw JSONL file content (trusted disk read) — not user-supplied request body data. The response is JSON-serialised via JSON.stringify() before transmission; the content field is a string embedded in a JSON object, preventing XSS injection through the API boundary."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-07-24T08:53:40Z",
          "note": "A04 — Insecure Design: PASS. The .md filename is derived entirely server-side from the validated .jsonl filename (filename.replace(/\\.jsonl$/, '.md')). This is the correct design — the client never supplies the cache path. The write-error-swallowing pattern (bare catch block on writeFile) is documented and intentional; it ensures a disk permission error on the cache write cannot take down the read path. No logic flaws in the security-critical flow identified."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-07-24T08:53:40Z",
          "note": "A05 — Security Misconfiguration: PASS. All API responses use sendJson() which applies the full securityHeaders() set: X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff, X-Frame-Options: DENY, Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin, Content-Security-Policy (default-src 'self'). CORS is restricted to localhost:{port}. Error messages use generic codes (NOT_FOUND, INTERNAL_ERROR) — file system details are not leaked to callers. Internal details are logged only to stderr."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-07-24T08:53:40Z",
          "note": "A06 — Vulnerable & Outdated Components: PASS (scoped). No new third-party dependencies were introduced by this WP. renderChunksToText() is an internal function from chunk-renderer.ts. The only Node.js builtins used are node:fs/promises (readFile, writeFile) and node:path (resolve, join, sep). No CVE check was triggered."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-07-24T08:53:40Z",
          "note": "A07 — Identification & Authentication Failures: N/A. This is a local-only GUI server with no user authentication layer. The endpoint is a read-only text extraction route. No session, token, or credential handling occurs."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-07-24T08:53:40Z",
          "note": "A08 — Software & Data Integrity Failures: PASS. The .md cache is written from renderChunksToText() output only — the function processes trusted on-disk JSONL content, not external input. No deserialization of untrusted data occurs. The caching mechanism does not introduce an integrity risk: a corrupted .md cache file would simply be returned as-is (cached: true) — this is an availability/correctness concern, not a security one."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-07-24T08:53:40Z",
          "note": "A09 — Security Logging & Monitoring Failures: PASS. Rejected filenames are logged via console.warn() with the literal (regex-validated-to-be-safe) input echoed in brackets. The warning prefixes ([handleGetChunkText]) are consistent with the existing codebase convention. Filesystem errors that propagate (non-ENOENT on readFile) are caught by the global error handler and logged to stderr. The write-error-swallowing catch block is the one case where an error is deliberately not logged — this is an identified low-priority observation (see below)."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-07-24T08:53:40Z",
          "note": "A10 — Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF): PASS. No outbound HTTP requests are made by this handler. All filesystem access is limited to paths under the resolveProjectStore()-returned storageDir. There is no URL fetching, DNS lookup, or proxy behaviour."
        },
        {
          "type": "risk",
          "priority": "medium",
          "timestamp": "2026-07-24T08:53:40Z",
          "note": "Medium — A09 / Observability Gap: The writeFile best-effort catch block (api.ts ~L1630) is intentionally bare and swallows all errors silently. In a scenario where the chunks directory has unexpected permissions (e.g. after a deployment change or container image rebuild), every cache-miss request would silently fail to cache, causing repeated JSONL re-extraction with no operator signal. Recommendation: add a console.warn() or stderr.write() inside the catch block (e.g. '[handleGetChunkText] Cache write failed for {mdPath}: {err.message}') so disk permission issues are observable in server logs without breaking the read path. This does not block approval — the read path is unaffected — but operators would have no signal of a persistent cache-write failure in production."
        },
        {
          "type": "risk",
          "priority": "medium",
          "timestamp": "2026-07-24T08:53:40Z",
          "note": "Medium — A05 / Information Disclosure (marginal): The console.warn() calls for rejected filenames (api.ts L1585, L1598) echo the raw filename string in the log message (e.g. 'Rejected filename (regex check): \\'../secret.jsonl\\''). Since CHUNK_FILENAME_RE has already failed at that point, the echoed string could contain attacker-controlled characters (path separators, null bytes, ANSI escape sequences). In a scenario where server logs are forwarded to a log aggregator UI, this could allow log injection or terminal escape injection. Recommendation: sanitize or truncate the filename before logging (e.g. JSON.stringify(filename.slice(0, 64)) to neutralize special chars). Low operational risk in a localhost-only server, but a good hardening measure. Does not block approval."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-07-24T08:53:40Z",
          "note": "Low — Input Validation / CHUNK_FILENAME_RE upper-bound: The allowlist regex /^[A-Za-z0-9_-]+\\.jsonl$/ has no maximum length constraint on the filename stem. A client could submit a 10,000-character filename that passes the regex, triggering a readFile() on a path that will not exist (benign ENOENT + 404), but consuming path-resolution overhead. Recommendation: add an early length check (e.g. if filename.length > 256) before the regex test, consistent with the MAX_SEGMENT_LENGTH=128 pattern used for slugs. Minor hardening — does not block approval."
        },
        {
          "type": "improvement",
          "priority": "low",
          "timestamp": "2026-07-24T08:53:40Z",
          "note": "Low — A08 / .md cache poisoning via symlink: If an attacker with local filesystem access places a symlink at the expected mdPath location pointing to a sensitive file, the cache-first readFile() at L1604 would read and return the symlink target's contents. The CHUNK_FILENAME_RE + prefix check prevents the client from controlling which mdPath is chosen, so this requires pre-existing local filesystem write access to the chunks directory — at which point the system is already compromised. Noted for completeness; not a remotely exploitable vector in the threat model of a localhost server."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "code-review",
      "status": "IN_PROGRESS",
      "started_at": "2026-07-24T08:54:10Z",
      "summary": []
